      "track": "section-b",
      "title": "Question 2",
      "markdown": "In Heung Shing, the coin system has n different denominations. We use an integer array c[0..n-1] (C/C++: c[n]) to represent the coin system, so that the n types of coins are worth, respectively, c[0] cents, c[1] cents, . . ., and c[n-1] cents, where 1 = c[0] < c[1] < · · · < c[n-1].\n\nAlice wants to solve the coin changing problem: what is the minimum number of coins needed to make a change of exactly m cents? She proposes the following greedy algorithm: first use as many as possible the most valuable coin, then use as many as possible the second most valuable coin, and so on."
    },
    {
      "id": "segments",
      "track": "section-b",
      "title": "Question 7",
      "markdown": "Given a function f that takes the coordinates of two horizontal line segments as parameters, where:\n\n- line segment a connects two points (ax1, ay) and (ax2, ay) and it is guaranteed that ax1 ≤ ax2.\n- line segment b connects two points (bx1, by) and (bx2, by) and it is guaranteed that bx1 ≤ bx2."
    }
